        "reasoning": "The problem asks for the least positive integer $x$ that satisfies the following system of linear congruences:\n    1. $x \\equiv 17 \\pmod{97}$\n    2. $x \\equiv 31 \\pmod{101}$\n    3. $x \\equiv 43 \\pmod{103}$\n\n    The moduli are $m_1 = 97$, $m_2 = 101$, and $m_3 = 103$.\n    Since 97, 101, and 103 are all prime numbers, they are pairwise coprime.\n    The Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) guarantees a unique solution modulo $M = 97 \\times 101 \\times 103$.\n\n    $M = 97 \\times 101 \\times 103$\n    $97 \\times 101 = 9797$\n    $9797 \\times 103 = 9797 \\times (100 + 3) = 979700 + 29391 = 1009091$.\n\n    The general solution is $x = \\sum a_i M_i y_i \\pmod M$, where:\n    $a_1 = 17, M_1 = 101 \\times 103 = 10403$\n    $a_2 = 31, M_2 = 97 \\times 103 = 9991$\n    $a_3 = 43, M_3 = 97 \\times 101 = 9797$\n    And $y_i$ is the modular inverse of $M_i$ modulo $m_i$.\n\n    *   **Step 4a: Find $y_1$ such that $10403 y_1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{97}$**\n        $10403 \\div 97$:\n        $10403 = 97 \\times 107 + 24$\n        So, $24 y_1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{97}$.\n        Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm:\n        $97 = 4 \\times 24 + 1$\n        $1 = 97 - 4 \\times 24$\n        So, $24(-4) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{97}$.\n        $y_1 = -4 \\equiv 93 \\pmod{97}$.\n\n    *   **Step 4b: Find $y_2$ such that $9991 y_2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{101}$**\n        $9991 \\div 101$:\n        $9991 = 101 \\times 98 + 93$\n        So, $93 y_2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{101}$.\n        $93 \\equiv -8 \\pmod{101}$.\n        $-8 y_2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{101}$.\n        $101 = 12 \\times 8 + 5$\n        $8 = 1 \\times 5 + 3$\n        $5 = 1 \\times 3 + 2$\n        $3 = 1 \\times 2 + 1$\n        Back-substituting:\n        $1 = 3 - 2 = 3 - (5 - 3) = 2 \\times 3 - 5 = 2 \\times (8 - 5) - 5 = 2 \\times 8 - 3 \\times 5$\n        $1 = 2 \\times 8 - 3 \\times (101 - 12 \\times 8) = 2 \\times 8 - 3 \\times 101 + 36 \\times 8 = 38 \\times 8 - 3 \\times 101$.\n        So, $8 \\times 38 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{101}$.\n        Since we have $-8 y_2 \\equiv 1$, then $y_2 \\equiv -38 \\equiv 63 \\pmod{101}$.\n\n    *   **Step 4c: Find $y_3$ such that $9797 y_3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{103}$**\n        $9797 \\div 103$:\n        $9797 = 103 \\times 95 + 12$\n        So, $12 y_3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{103}$.\n        $103 = 8 \\times 12 + 7$\n        $12 = 1 \\times 7 + 5$\n        $7 = 1 \\times 5 + 2$\n        $5 = 2 \\times 2 + 1$\n        Back-substituting:\n        $1 = 5 - 2 \\times 2 = 5 - 2 \\times (7 - 5) = 3 \\times 5 - 2 \\times 7 = 3 \\times (12 - 7) - 2 \\times 7 = 3 \\times 12 - 5 \\times 7$\n        $1 = 3 \\times 12 - 5 \\times (103 - 8 \\times 12) = 3 \\times 12 - 5 \\times 103 + 40 \\times 12 = 43 \\times 12 - 5 \\times 103$.\n        So, $12 \\times 43 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{103}$.\n        $y_3 = 43 \\pmod{103}$.\n\n    $x = (a_1 M_1 y_1) + (a_2 M_2 y_2) + (a_3 M_3 y_3) \\pmod M$\n    $x = (17 \\times 10403 \\times 93) + (31 \\times 9991 \\times 63) + (43 \\times 9797 \\times 43) \\pmod{1009091}$\n\n    Wait, this is getting very large. Let's try a different approach (Iterative substitution).\n\n    1. $x = 97k + 17$\n    2. $97k + 17 \\equiv 31 \\pmod{101}$\n       $97k \\equiv 14 \\pmod{101}$\n       $-4k \\equiv 14 \\pmod{101}$\n       $-2k \\equiv 7 \\pmod{101}$\n       $-2k \\equiv 108 \\pmod{101}$\n       $k \\equiv -54 \\equiv 47 \\pmod{101}$\n       So, $k = 101m + 47$.\n\n    3. Substitute $k$ back into $x$:\n       $x = 97(101m + 47) + 17$\n       $x = 9797m + (97 \\times 47) + 17$\n       $97 \\times 47 = 97 \\times (50 - 3) = 4850 - 291 = 4559$.\n       $x = 9797m + 4559 + 17 = 9797m + 4576$.\n\n    4. Now use the third congruence:\n       $x \\equiv 43 \\pmod{103}$\n       $9797m + 4576 \\equiv 43 \\pmod{103}$\n       First, reduce the coefficients modulo 103:\n       $9797 \\div 103$: $9797 = 103 \\times 95 + 12$. So $9797 \\equiv 12 \\pmod{103}$.\n       $4576 \\div 103$: $4576 = 103 \\times 44 + 44$. So $4576 \\equiv 44 \\pmod{103}$.\n       $12m + 44 \\equ